<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">South Saskatchewan River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a> River is formed in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberta">Alberta</a> by the meeting of the Bow and Oldman rivers and flows through Saskatchewan, past Saskatoon, to join the North Saskatchewan east of Prince Albert. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> families settled river lots along its banks around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batoche">Batoche</a> and St. Laurent in the 1870s, and the fighting of 1885 took place in its valley.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/south-saskatchewan-river.jpg" alt="South Saskatchewan River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photograph by Dean Shareski (shareski (Flickr) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_Saskatchewan_River.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The South Saskatchewan River begins in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberta">Alberta</a>, at the confluence of the Bow and Oldman Rivers north of Grassy Lake, and ends at the Saskatchewan River Forks in central Saskatchewan, where it joins the North Saskatchewan River to form the Saskatchewan River. Both of its source rivers draw on winter snowpack and rainfall in the Rocky Mountains near the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a>. Glacier and snowmelt from the Rocky Mountains and foothills supply nearly 90 per cent of the annual flow, most of it in July and August.</p><p>From the headwaters of the Bow River the South Saskatchewan flows for 1,392 kilometres. At its mouth it has an average discharge of 280 cubic metres per second and a watershed of 146,100 square kilometres, of which 1,800 lie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a>. The Red Deer River is its major tributary, joining 16 kilometres east of the Alberta-Saskatchewan border.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For the first half of the 20th century the river froze over completely in winter, and its ice breaks made conditions spectacular and dangerous at Saskatoon, Medicine Hat and elsewhere; at least one bridge in Saskatoon was destroyed by ice. The Gardiner Dam, built in the 1960s, lessened the river's power by diverting a substantial part of its flow into the Qu'Appelle River, and by the 1980s many permanent sandbars had formed as the level fell. The dams created the reservoir of Lake Diefenbaker.</p><p>Sections of the riverbank are prone to slumping, and Saskatoon has dealt with a number of slope failures since its founding; the control of riverbank development was a factor in the establishment of the Meewasin Valley Authority in 1979.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Below the dam the river flows north through Saskatoon, and the Meewasin Valley Authority is responsible for conserving the river environment for about 60 kilometres near the city. Oxbows of the river formed several lakes there, among them Moon Lake and Pike Lake. Four Important Bird Areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> lie along the river, two at Lake Diefenbaker. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a> section the valley floor is heavily wooded, with eastern cottonwood, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> maple and green ash, and its birds include the piping plover and the great blue heron. The fish of the river include walleye, sauger, northern pike, goldeye and lake sturgeon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “South Saskatchewan River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Métis families who had moved west from Red River to the <b>South Saskatchewan River</b> after 1870 feared losing their river lots to surveys, while Cree nations faced hunger after the buffalo vanished.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-saskatchewan">Provisional Government of Saskatchewan</a></li></ul></section>
